A||||9||Lisa 86 2m4||Speaker install problem...||||1||
Z||000000||Lisa 86 2m4||02-09-2001||07:19 PM||||Hi everyone!<BR>I don't know what it is, but every time I embark on what should be a simple proceedure....I just got new 4X10's for the dash, but the hook up is arranged differently than the stock.  The stock has a little a/b clip for the wiring.  I thought it would be easiest to take the clip off the stock, un-solder the new speakers then solder on the clip....but I can't seem the get teh wires out of the clip- even though with my dremel I cut through every piece of metal... am I at least in the right direction?  Is there a trick I'm missing?  If I break the brackets off the clip thingy will that help?  And will the connection be ok if it's not attached to the final speaker, but hangs by the speaker wire?<BR>Thanks for any help you can impart!!!<BR>Lisa<BR>Feeling like a total blonde!||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||02-09-2001||07:43 PM||||The two most common methods are: 1) buy some inexpensive speaker adapter terminals, and 2) use the connectors that came with your speaker and splice them into the factory wires.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/batkid3.gif">    <B>BATBOY</B><BR>1985 SE auto with 350 Chevy V8 (Batmobile),<BR>suspension mods, lowered, GA brakes, battery<BR>relocated up front, stereo mods, etc...||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||02-09-2001||08:01 PM||||i used method 2  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>if you want to get some adapters, crutchfield sells them. <A HREF="http://www.crutchfield.com" TARGET=_blank>www.crutchfield.com</A> <P>BTW, what kind of 4x10s ya got?<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Lisa 86 2m4||02-09-2001||08:25 PM||||For some reason I was thinking it would be just sooo bad to cut my factory wires...Trying to make more work for myself, I guess.<BR>I just got cheapy Pyramid 4018Golds, but they look pretty decent...for 10 years I've been happy with stock so I'm sure these are better yet.<BR>Thanks for the advice! I needed it!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000004||MrPBody||02-09-2001||10:08 PM||||The plastic connectors have a little latch holding them on the stock speakers.  I was able to pry-up the latch, slide the connectors off, and solder the braided wires onto the new speaker connectors.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||02-10-2001||09:24 AM||||If you don't want to cut the factory wires, then get the adapters. I bought a pair at Best Buy. Most retail car audio shops carry them too.<P>Many of us have modded our cars so much that snipping speaker wires is pretty minor. The choice is yours.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/batkid3.gif">    <B>BATBOY</B><BR>1985 SE auto with 350 Chevy V8 (Batmobile),<BR>suspension mods, lowered, GA brakes, battery<BR>relocated up front, stereo mods, etc...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Lisa 86 2m4||02-10-2001||01:31 PM||||Thanks all for the input. I braved it out and just cut, spliced and soldered.  It was much easier.  Thanks for letting me know that it was ok to do that.!  They don't sound too, bad either, maybe a little muffeley.  Next up, maybe put in new 4X6's. Unless anyone has other suggestions  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I can do the work, as long as I know what can be done, ya know? Any advise on what specs to look for in speakers? Like the ones I just put in were 4 ohms (Whatever that means!!) and yada, yada.  I love advice!!<BR>Thanks everyone!<BR>Lisa||||reg||10
Z||000007||sqoach||02-10-2001||01:45 PM||||here's some advice, don't get speakers that sound "muffeley"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>actually there is not a speaker for everyone. it's just personal opinion. (oreif, i'm stealing your lines  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<BR>a lot (most) of the speakers you find will be 4 ohm. i'm not exactly sure what it means either, but i know older speakers were 8 ohm, so 4 must be better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> when you get some 4x6s, make sure the mounting depth is under 1 3/4" or it won't fit very good. i had a set that were 2" mounting depth, and they didn't fit very well.<BR>and here's another word of advice: you get what you pay for. most cases this is correct. if you buy a cheap speaker, you'll get cheap sound. (cheap sound?? you get the point)||||reg||10
Z||000008||batboy||02-10-2001||04:31 PM||||Lisa, I'd recommend buying the same brand and model speakers for front and back. When you go to replace the rear speakers, you'll have to remove that whole inner sail panel and shoulder harness mount.<P>Lisa, I hope you don't take offense to this, but if I were you and were able to scrape up a little more money, I'd return those wimpy Pyramid speakers and get some better ones. Choices are limited with 4"X10" speakers, but I think the Pioneer coaxials sound really good. I have a pair of them in my Batmobile. Also, I had a complete set of Kickers in my first Fiero and they really rocked. I can give you a couple URL links to car audio wholesalers if you want. Regardless, like you said, anything will be better than the old crappy factory speakers.||||reg||1
Z||000009||RossT||02-10-2001||07:33 PM||||Lisa, read this link, it will help.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005268.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005268.html</A> <P>I just replaced all four speakers in mine.||||reg||1
